  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1143 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nPrimary concern remains location and timing of thunderstorms\nWednesday. CAMs have been in decent agreement in developing\nstorms over central MO in the pre-dawn hours, near a 925mb warm\nfront. 00Z SGF sounding showed a warm layer near 700mb that will\nneed to be displaced by height falls overnight. Post-00Z runs of\nthe HRRR have turned dry, but most of the CAMs, including the\nHREF and REFS, suggest storms will initiate southwest of St.\nLouis, and move into the bootheel and northwest TN by midmorning.\n00Z HRRR soundings over west TN depict DCAPE less than 700 J/kg,\nbut MLCAPE in excess of 2000 J/kg by noon. 35-40KT of deep layer\nshear should help storms stay organized, assuming they develop\nupstream earlier in the morning.\n\nMLCAPE should recover though late afternoon, particularly along\nand west of the MS River. CAMs have come into better alignment\ndepicting the bulk of storms passing central AR, with a few\nstorms tracking through eastern AR, southwest TN and northwest\nMS. DCAPE will be more favorable in the late afternoon and\nevening, approaching 1000 J/kg over eastern AR. Storms should\nexit MS late Wednesday evening. A few elevated storms may follow\nlate Wednesday night east of the MS River, associated with the\npassage of a midlevel trough. A weak stationary front may serve\nas a focus for isolated afternoon thunderstorms over northeast MS\nand areas of TN near the TN River. Latest NBM guidance suggests\nmostly clear skies for the late Thursday evening lunar eclipse,\nthough clouds may linger over northeast MS.\n\nHot and dry weather appears on the near horizon, as an upper\nridge over NM and AZ expands eastward into the Midsouth by\nSunday. We remain in close proximity to the ridge center through\nmidweek, with daytime temperatures warming to the upper 90s and\nheat indices 100-105, slightly higher over the delta.",
